on/re-repair (Tables 12-13)\n- Section 6: Discussion — architecture dependence, repo-level limitations, CWM token budget caveat, negative re-repair result\n- Section 7: Conclusion\n- References: 6 entries (Gu et al. 2024, Jimenez et al. 2024, Li 2025, Ni et al. 2024, Shi et al. 2024, Zhang et al. 2024, Zhang et al. 2025)\n\n## Key numbers\n- Function-level: CLS 88.43-89.40%, CWM 86.96-88.21%, gaps 0.52-2.43pp\n- Repo-level CLS: 79.88% mean (3-seed), majority baseline 72.96%\n- Repo-level CWM: max 75.5% at 2048 tokens, 35× cost\n- Oracle ensemble: 90.63% (4B) to 93.1% (8B)\n- Re-repair: +0.5pp (p=0.72), not significant\n- Dataset: SWE-bench-PerTest, 458 instances, 71.2% T=1, pass rate 73%\n\n## Your task\n\nEvaluate the **novelty, significance, related work, and presentation** of this paper. Provide:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on novelty/significance/related work/writing)\n4. **Questions for authors** (numbered)\n5. **Score** (1-6) and **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider:\n- Is the research question novel and well-motivated?\n- Are the contributions significant enough for a top venue?\n- Is the related work coverage adequate? Are there important missing references?\n- Does the paper position itself clearly against prior work?\n- Is the writing clear and well-organized?\n- Are the claims well-calibrated to the evidence?\n- Is the \"complementarity\" thesis genuinely novel or somewhat obvious?\n- Does the paper read as a complete contribution or a preliminary study?\n- The references are very sparse (only 6 entries) — is this adequate?\n- Are the reference formats complete and correct? (Note: some entries say \"and 1 others\" which is unusual)\n\nOutput your review in markdown format. Be specific and cite sections/tables."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01A559eZPhoFF4kZVPkScFsS","session_id":"d1877127-5380-427b-bef7-ba16c8f39205","uuid":"0646131f-f630-4a0a-8288-b4019c3b46ab","timestamp":"