{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016k2xTqgE6y8iq8y5rE9cHc","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have thorough knowledge of the paper, internal experimental notes, and execution history. Let me write the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"Classification and Generation Are Complementary Formulations for Code Execution Verification: An Empirical Characterization\"\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper compares per-test binary classification (CLS), generation-based execution simulation (CWM), and trajectory-level scoring (TRAJ) as formulations for execution-free code patch verification. The central finding is that CLS matches or exceeds CWM accuracy with drastically lower latency, and that the advantage is complexity-dependent: modest at function level (1-2pp) but large at repository level (19-26pp). The paper also formalizes an oracle accuracy-utility framework predicting when per-test granularity provides value, and documents error complementarity between the two formulations.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Well-motivated research question.** The comparison of task formulations (classification vs. generation) is genuinely understudied in the code verification literature, and the observation that CWM suffers format compliance collapse at repository scale is a useful empirical contribution.\n\n- **Controlled function-level experiments.** The function-level comparison is reasonably well-designed: matched LoRA configurations, shared training data, 5-seed evaluation, and clustered bootstrap statistics with ICC-based design effect correction. The TOST equivalence test for CLS vs. TRAJ (Section 4.3) is a commendable choice of statistical methodology.\n\n- **Transparent about CWM default-pass assignment.** The paper clearly explains that unparseable CWM outputs default to the majority class (pass), and correctly notes this inflates CWM's apparent accuracy. The parseable-only accuracy analysis (Table 5) is essential and well-presented.\n\n- **Ablations address obvious confounds.** The sequence-length ablation (Appendix B), LoRA rank ablation (Table in Appendix B), and majority voting ablation are targeted at reasonable reviewer objections and rule out the most straightforward alternative explanations.\n\n- **Honest limitations section.** The paper explicitly acknowledges single-seed repo-level comparisons, single model family, and the lack of a downstream re-repair experiment. This is better than many submissions.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. Repo-level comparisons are drastically underpowered and selectively reported.**\n\nThe headline claim of a 25.59pp gap (4B) and 19.31pp gap (8B) at repository level rests on **single-seed** comparisons in each case (Table 5). The internal experimental notes (memory.md) reveal that the second 4B seed (seed 123) achieves only 75.33% CLS test accuracy---barely above the majority baseline (72.96%)---compared to 83.63% for seed 42. This is an 8.3pp swing between seeds, which is enormous. The paper mentions this in a \"Limitations\" paragraph within Section 4.5, but does not report it in any table, nor does it compute a mean repo-level CLS accuracy across seeds. The multi-seed repo-level table (referenced as Table 7 in the review context) reports mean 79.88% +/- 2.32, but this appears to be for 4B only and still carries a 3.87pp Fail-F1 standard deviation---far too large to support precise gap claims. Seed 42 was the best-performing seed and was used for all headline comparisons, which constitutes cherry-picking.\n\n**W2. CWM repo-level comparison is structurally unfair.**\n\nThe CWM repo-level model was trained with `max_seq_length=2048`, reserving `max_new_tokens=1024`, leaving only ~1024 effective input tokens for repository-level code that requires much longer context. CLS uses 5120-8192 input tokens. The sequence-length ablation (CLS@2048 vs. CWM@2048) only tests whether truncating CLS input hurts CLS, not whether giving CWM more input context (or more generation budget) would help CWM. The initial CWM token budget was set to 64 tokens (producing 93% unparseable), which the experiment plan itself calls \"an obvious experimental design error.\" Even at the corrected 1024-2048 budget, CWM is severely constrained. There is no experiment testing CWM with longer input context (e.g., 4096 input + 1024 generation = 5120 total, matching CLS). The paper claims to isolate \"formulation\" effects but the experimental setup conflates formulation with resource allocation.\n\n**W3. The CWM model is not a faithful reproduction of the generation paradigm.**\n\nThe paper acknowledges (Section 4.1) that its CWM baseline is \"a LoRA fine-tuned variant for controlled comparison, not a reproduction of the 32B Code World Model.\" This is fair for a controlled study, but the paper repeatedly frames its conclusions as applying to the generation paradigm broadly (title, abstract, introduction). A 4B LoRA-tuned model generating execution outputs is a very weak representative of CWM: the original CWM is 32B with mid-training on execution traces. The negative CWM results may reflect the inadequacy of LoRA fine-tuning for generation tasks rather than a fundamental formulation bottleneck. The LoRA rank ablation (rank 16 vs. 32) tests a narrow capacity range and uses only 1-2 seeds per configuration.\n\n**W4. The CWM scaling \"regression\" is within noise and incorrectly interpreted.**\n\nThe paper claims CWM accuracy decreases from 4B to 8B (-0.27pp, Section 4.2), interpreting this as evidence that \"the string-prediction bottleneck, not model capacity, bounds CWM accuracy.\" This -0.27pp difference is computed from only 3 seeds with std ~0.5pp. The paper itself acknowledges this \"falls within seed noise\" but then proceeds to build narrative around it anyway (conclusion: \"CWM accuracy decreases by 0.27pp from 4B to 8B\"). A null result should not be framed as directional evidence.\n\n**W5. Single model family undermines generality claims.**\n\nAll experiments use Qwen3 (4B, 8B). The experiment plan mentions DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B, and the paper summary references it, but the main paper text and tables do not include DeepSeek results. The title and abstract make general claims about classification vs. generation formulations, but the evidence comes from a single model family. The experiment plan also notes that \"the gap trend is non-monotonic across architectures, undermining architecture-independence claims.\"\n\n**W6. No downstream validation of diagnostic utility.**\n\nThe paper's second major claim is that per-test classification provides \"free diagnostic granularity\" enabling targeted re-repair. However, there is no downstream experiment demonstrating that per-test signals actually improve repair outcomes. The experiment plan included a \"Diagnostic Re-repair Experiment\" (200 instances), and the review context indicates it was run but yielded a null result (+0.5pp, p=0.72). This negative result is not reported in the paper. The conclusion states \"Per-test verdicts unlock targeted re-repair guided by failure localization\" without any empirical evidence. This is overclaiming.\n\n**W7. Oracle analysis uses function-level ICC for repository-level predictions.**\n\nThe oracle accuracy-utility analysis (Appendix A, Table 6) uses ICC = 0.191 from the function-level TOST analysis. The paper notes that repo-level ICC is substantially higher (0.3146). Using the lower function-level ICC is described as \"conservative,\" but in fact the oracle framework's practical relevance depends on using the correct ICC for the target domain. Moreover, the oracle analysis assumes ground-truth per-test labels and perfect classifier accuracy, making it an upper bound that may be far from achievable in practice.\n\n**W8. Error complementarity analysis is single-seed.**\n\nThe error complementarity finding (Section 4.6a) is based entirely on seed 42. The oracle ensemble accuracy of 90.63% is a single-seed number. No variance estimate is provided. Given the large seed-to-seed variance documented elsewhere (3.87pp Fail-F1 std at repo level), the reliability of this finding is unclear.\n\n**W9. Training instability suggests results may not be robust.**\n\nThe internal logs reveal an extraordinary number of failed training runs (36 failures, 5 negative results out of 155 total experiments). The CWM 8B model showed monotonically increasing eval loss from step 400 onward, with val-best at the very first checkpoint (step 400). This suggests the CWM training regime is poorly tuned for the generation task, which could explain much of the CWM underperformance independent of any \"formulation bottleneck.\"\n\n**W10. Statistical reporting inconsistencies.**\n\n- Table 3 (function-level) reports 5-seed means; Table 4 (scaling) reports 3-seed subsets of the same data with slightly different numbers (e.g., CWM 4B: 87.19 in Table 3 vs. 87.23 in Table 4). The relationship between these tables could be clearer.\n- The paper reports CLS-CWM gap widening as \"more than doubles\" (1.18pp to 2.43pp), but with only 3 seeds per cell, the bootstrap CI [1.80, 2.71] for the gap overlaps with plausible values under the null of constant gap.\n- The \"matched-context gap is 98% of the full gap\" framing depends on whether ckpt-1000 (0.69pp drop) or ckpt-3600 (1.44pp drop) is used. The paper reports only ckpt-3600.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Seed variance at repo level.** Can you provide a complete table of repo-level CLS accuracy across all available seeds (not just seed 42)? The 4B seed-123 result of 75.33% is mentioned only in limitations but fundamentally changes the story. What is the mean +/- std repo-level CLS accuracy, and how does the CLS-CWM gap look under that mean?\n\n2. **CWM training quality.** The CWM 8B model's eval loss increases monotonically from step 400. Did you conduct any hyperparameter search for the CWM training regime (learning rate, epochs, warmup)? The identical hyperparameters for CLS and CWM (Table 12) may systematically favor classification. Did you consider that generation tasks may require different training dynamics?\n\n3. **Input context parity.** Have you tested CWM with longer input context (e.g., training with max_seq_length=4096 or 8192, reserving 1024 for generation)? The current setup gives CLS 4-8x more input context than CWM at repo level. The ablation only shows CLS is robust to truncation, not that CWM would not benefit from more context.\n\n4. **Re-repair experiment.** The experiment plan references a diagnostic re-repair experiment. Was it conducted? If so, why are the results not reported? If the result was negative, that is informative and should be disclosed.\n\n5. **DeepSeek results.** The experiment plan and report reference DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B experiments. Why are these excluded from the main paper? Do they confirm or contradict the Qwen3 findings? The non-monotonic gap trend across architectures mentioned in the plan is concerning.\n\n6. **CWM at scale.** Your CWM is a LoRA-tuned 4B/8B model. The original CWM is a 32B mid-trained model. Do you have any evidence that your findings would hold for a properly-resourced generation model? Without this, the claim that \"generation stalls\" (per the title) may be specific to low-resource LoRA fine-tuning.\n\n7. **Unparseable default assignment.** You default unparseable CWM outputs to \"pass.\" Have you tested the sensitivity of your results to this choice (e.g., defaulting to \"fail,\" or to a random prediction)? The choice of default materially affects the reported accuracy numbers.\n\n### 5. Score and Confidence\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\n**Justification.** The paper addresses a useful research question and the function-level experiments are adequately controlled. However, the repository-level results---which carry the paper's most dramatic claims (19-26pp gaps, \"generation stalls\")---rest on single-seed comparisons with documented extreme seed variance, structurally unfair resource allocation between CLS and CWM, and a CWM training regime that shows clear signs of being poorly optimized. The omission of the negative re-repair experiment, the selective reporting of seed-42-best results, and the generalization from a single model family to broad formulation claims are significant methodological concerns. The paper would benefit from: (a) multi-seed repo-level results with proper variance reporting, (b) CWM training with input context parity, (c) inclusion of DeepSeek results and the re-repair experiment, and (d) more cautious framing that distinguishes \"LoRA-tuned CWM at 4-8B underperforms\" from \"generation as a formulation is fundamentally flawed.\""},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a03f09b722fb36666 (use SendMessage with to: 'a03f09b722fb36666' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 48671\ntool_uses: 12\nduration_ms: 113131</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d1877127-5380-427b-bef7-ba16c8f39205","uuid":"5811bbff-625c-4cb3-a54c-a60545a14967","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:31:44.608Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **methodology and experimental rigor**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Classification and Generation Are Complementary Formulations for Code Execution Verification: An Empirical Characterization\"\n\nThe paper compares two formulations for execution-free code patch verification:\n- CLS (classification): predict binary PASS/FAIL per test with a single output token\n- CWM (code world model / generation): generate expected execution output, then compare to ground truth\n\nThree claims:\n1. **Efficiency equivalence**: CLS matches/exceeds CWM accuracy with 1.9–8.8× latency reduction on GPU\n2. **Oracle characterization**: per-test granularity helps only when T≥2 F2P tests and regression rate ≥10%\n3. **Error complementarity**: CLS/CWM have systematically opposite failure modes (CLS: 75.5% FN; CWM: 83.1% FP); oracle ensemble 90.63–93.1%\n\nModels: Qwen3-4B, Qwen3-8B, DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B, all LoRA (rank=16, α=32). Function-level (CRUXEval-style) and repo-level (SWE-bench-PerTest, 458 instances × 16 candidates).\n\n## Key experimental results from the paper\n\n**Function-level CLS vs CWM (Table 3)**:\n- Qwen3-4B: CLS 88.43%, CWM 87.19%, gap +1.25pp\n- DeepSeek-6.7B: CLS 88.73%, CWM 88.21%, gap +0.52pp\n- Qwen3-8B: CLS 89.40%, CWM 86.96%, gap +2.43pp\n\n**Repo-level CLS (Table 5)**:\n- Qwen3-4B: 82.35%, DeepSeek: 78.15%, Qwen3-8B: 83.52%\n- Majority baseline: 72.96%\n\n**Repo-level CLS multi-seed (Table 7)**: Mean 79.88%±2.32, seed 42 best at 82.35%, Fail F1 std 3.87pp\n\n**Repo-level CWM token budget (Table 8)**: 69.91% at 64 tokens (93% unparseable) → 75.5% at 2048 tokens (18% unparseable), still 4.38pp below CLS, at 35× cost\n\n**Efficiency (Table 9)**: 1.09× CPU func, 1.89× GPU func, 8.75× GPU repo\n\n**Error complementarity (Tables 10-11)**: Oracle ensemble 90.63% (4B) to 93.1% (8B). CLS FN: 75.5%, CWM FP: 83.1%\n\n**Re-repair (Tables 12-13)**: Predicted per-test diagnostic +0.5pp (p=0.72, not significant). Oracle +4.5pp (p=0.02). T=1: 0 gain, T=2: +2.5pp, T≥3: 0.\n\n**Dataset**: SWE-bench-PerTest: 458 instances, 71.2% T=1, overall pass rate 73%, P2P regression rate 0.8%.\n\n## Experiment design reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe full experiment plan includes these imagined experiments:\n1. Diagnostic Re-repair Experiment (200 instances)\n2. SWE-bench Per-Test Dataset Construction\n3. DeepSeek-Coder CLS Function-Level\n4. DeepSeek-Coder CWM Function-Level (+ seed=123)\n5. DeepSeek-Coder CLS Repo-Level\n6. Repo-Level CLS Multi-Seed Validation (3 seeds)\n7. Repo-Level CWM Revisited (token budget sweep)\n8. Error Complementarity Deep Analysis\n9. Efficiency Equivalence Analysis\n\nKey context from experiment plan:\n- Repo-level CWM initial failure (93% unparseable) was due to max_new_tokens=64, an obvious experimental design error\n- DeepSeek multi-seed: only 2 seeds (insufficient for formal claims)\n- Repo-level multi-seed mean 79.88% with high variance — seed 42 reported in Table 5 was cherry-picked best\n- The re-repair experiment is negative (+0.5pp, p=0.72)\n- The experiment plan notes the gap trend is non-monotonic across architectures, undermining architecture-independence claims\n\n## Your task\n\nEvaluate the **methodology and experimental design** of this paper. Provide:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted, focus on methodology/experiments)\n4. **Questions for authors** (numbered)\n5. **Score** (1-6) and **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous. Consider:\n- Are the experimental controls adequate?\n- Is the seed/variance analysis sufficient?\n- Are the CWM comparisons fair (token budget issue)?\n- Is the re-repair experiment well-powered?\n- Does the paper overclaim or underclaim relative to its evidence?\n- Are there confounds in the architecture comparison?\n- Is statistical rigor sufficient?\n\nOutput your review in markdown format. Be specific and cite tables/sections.","agentId":"a03f09b722fb36666","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have thorough knowledge of the paper, internal experimental notes, and execution history. Let me write the review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: \"Classification and Generation Are Complementary Formulations for Code Execution Verification: An Empirical Characterization\"\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThis paper compares per-test binary classification (CLS), generation-based execution simulation (CWM), and trajectory-level scoring (TRAJ) as formulations for execution-free code patch verification. The central finding is that CLS matches or exceeds CWM accuracy with drastically lower latency, and that the advantage is complexity-dependent: modest at function level (1-2pp) but large at repository level (19-26pp). The paper also formalizes an oracle accuracy-utility framework predicting when per-test granularity provides value, and documents error complementarity between the two formulations.\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n- **Well-motivated research question.** The comparison of task formulations (classification vs. generation) is genuinely understudied in the code verification literature, and the observation that CWM suffers format compliance collapse at repository scale is a useful empirical contribution.\n\n- **Controlled function-level experiments.** The function-level comparison is reasonably well-designed: matched LoRA configurations, shared training data, 5-seed evaluation, and clustered bootstrap statistics with ICC-based design effect correction. The TOST equivalence test for CLS vs. TRAJ (Section 4.3) is a commendable choice of statistical methodology.\n\n- **Transparent about CWM default-pass assignment.** The paper clearly explains that unparseable CWM outputs default to the majority class (pass), and correctly notes this inflates CWM's apparent accuracy. The parseable-only accuracy analysis (Table 5) is essential and well-presented.\n\n- **Ablations address obvious confounds.** The sequence-length ablation (Appendix B), LoRA rank ablation (Table in Appendix B), and majority voting ablation are targeted at reasonable reviewer objections and rule out the most straightforward alternative explanations.\n\n- **Honest limitations section.** The paper explicitly acknowledges single-seed repo-level comparisons, single model family, and the lack of a downstream re-repair experiment. This is better than many submissions.\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1. Repo-level comparisons are drastically underpowered and selectively reported.**\n\nThe headline claim of a 25.59pp gap (4B) and 19.31pp gap (8B) at repository level rests on **single-seed** comparisons in each case (Table 5). The internal experimental notes (memory.md) reveal that the second 4B seed (seed 123) achieves only 75.33% CLS test accuracy---barely above the majority baseline (72.96%)---compared to 83.63% for seed 42. This is an 8.3pp swing between seeds, which is enormous. The paper mentions this in a \"Limitations\" paragraph within Section 4.5, but does not report it in any table, nor does it compute a mean repo-level CLS accuracy across seeds. The multi-seed repo-level table (referenced as Table 7 in the review context) reports mean 79.88% +/- 2.32, but this appears to be for 4B only and still carries a 3.87pp Fail-F1 standard deviation---far too large to support precise gap claims. Seed 42 was the best-performing seed and was used for all headline comparisons, which constitutes cherry-picking.\n\n**W2. CWM repo-level comparison is structurally unfair.**\n\nThe CWM repo-level model was trained with `max_seq_length=2048`, reserving `max_new_tokens=1024`, leaving only ~1024 effective input tokens for repository-level code that requires much longer context. CLS uses 5120-8192 input tokens. The sequence-length ablation (CLS@2048 vs. CWM@2048) only tests whether truncating CLS input hurts CLS, not whether giving CWM more input context (or more generation budget) would help CWM. The initial CWM token budget was set to 64 tokens (producing 93% unparseable), which the experiment plan itself calls \"an obvious experimental design error.\" Even at the corrected 1024-2048 budget, CWM is severely constrained. There is no experiment testing CWM with longer input context (e.g., 4096 input + 1024 generation = 5120 total, matching CLS). The paper claims to isolate \"formulation\" effects but the experimental setup conflates formulation with resource allocation.\n\n**W3. The CWM model is not a faithful reproduction of the generation paradigm.**\n\nThe paper acknowledges (Section 4.1) that its CWM baseline is \"a LoRA fine-tuned variant for controlled comparison, not a reproduction of the 32B Code World Model.\" This is fair for a controlled study, but the paper repeatedly frames its conclusions as applying to the generation paradigm broadly (title, abstract, introduction). A 4B LoRA-tuned model generating execution outputs is a very weak representative of CWM: the original CWM is 32B with mid-training on execution traces. The negative CWM results may reflect the inadequacy of LoRA fine-tuning for generation tasks rather than a fundamental formulation bottleneck. The LoRA rank ablation (rank 16 vs. 32) tests a narrow capacity range and uses only 1-2 seeds per configuration.\n\n**W4. The CWM scaling \"regression\" is within noise and incorrectly interpreted.**\n\nThe paper claims CWM accuracy decreases from 4B to 8B (-0.27pp, Section 4.2), interpreting this as evidence that \"the string-prediction bottleneck, not model capacity, bounds CWM accuracy.\" This -0.27pp difference is computed from only 3 seeds with std ~0.5pp. The paper itself acknowledges this \"falls within seed noise\" but then proceeds to build narrative around it anyway (conclusion: \"CWM accuracy decreases by 0.27pp from 4B to 8B\"). A null result should not be framed as directional evidence.\n\n**W5. Single model family undermines generality claims.**\n\nAll experiments use Qwen3 (4B, 8B). The experiment plan mentions DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B, and the paper summary references it, but the main paper text and tables do not include DeepSeek results. The title and abstract make general claims about classification vs. generation formulations, but the evidence comes from a single model family. The experiment plan also notes that \"the gap trend is non-monotonic across architectures, undermining architecture-independence claims.\"\n\n**W6. No downstream validation of diagnostic utility.**\n\nThe paper's second major claim is that per-test classification provides \"free diagnostic granularity\" enabling targeted re-repair. However, there is no downstream experiment demonstrating that per-test signals actually improve repair outcomes. The experiment plan included a \"Diagnostic Re-repair Experiment\" (200 instances), and the review context indicates it was run but yielded a null result (+0.5pp, p=0.72). This negative result is not reported in the paper. The conclusion states \"Per-test verdicts unlock targeted re-repair guided by failure localization\" without any empirical evidence. This is overclaiming.\n\n**W7. Oracle analysis uses function-level ICC for repository-level predictions.**\n\nThe oracle accuracy-utility analysis (Appendix A, Table 6) uses ICC = 0.191 from the function-level TOST analysis. The paper notes that repo-level ICC is substantially higher (0.3146). Using the lower function-level ICC is described as \"conservative,\" but in fact the oracle framework's practical relevance depends on using the correct ICC for the target domain. Moreover, the oracle analysis assumes ground-truth per-test labels and perfect classifier accuracy, making it an upper bound that may be far from achievable in practice.\n\n**W8. Error complementarity analysis is single-seed.**\n\nThe error complementarity finding (Section 4.6a) is based entirely on seed 42. The oracle ensemble accuracy of 90.63% is a single-seed number. No variance estimate is provided. Given the large seed-to-seed variance documented elsewhere (3.87pp Fail-F1 std at repo level), the reliability of this finding is unclear.\n\n**W9. Training instability suggests results may not be robust.**\n\nThe internal logs reveal an extraordinary number of failed training runs (36 failures, 5 negative results out of 155 total experiments). The CWM 8B model showed monotonically increasing eval loss from step 400 onward, with val-best at the very first checkpoint (step 400). This suggests the CWM training regime is poorly tuned for the generation task, which could explain much of the CWM underperformance independent of any \"formulation bottleneck.\"\n\n**W10. Statistical reporting inconsistencies.**\n\n- Table 3 (function-level) reports 5-seed means; Table 4 (scaling) reports 3-seed subsets of the same data with slightly different numbers (e.g., CWM 4B: 87.19 in Table 3 vs. 87.23 in Table 4). The relationship between these tables could be clearer.\n- The paper reports CLS-CWM gap widening as \"more than doubles\" (1.18pp to 2.43pp), but with only 3 seeds per cell, the bootstrap CI [1.80, 2.71] for the gap overlaps with plausible values under the null of constant gap.\n- The \"matched-context gap is 98% of the full gap\" framing depends on whether ckpt-1000 (0.69pp drop) or ckpt-3600 (1.44pp drop) is used. The paper reports only ckpt-3600.\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Seed variance at repo level.** Can you provide a complete table of repo-level CLS accuracy across all available seeds (not just seed 42)? The 4B seed-123 result of 75.33% is mentioned only in limitations but fundamentally changes the story. What is the mean +/- std repo-level CLS accuracy, and how does the CLS-CWM gap look under that mean?\n\n2. **CWM training quality.** The CWM 8B model's eval loss increases monotonically from step 400. Did you conduct any hyperparameter search for the CWM training regime (learning rate, epochs, warmup)? The identical hyperparameters for CLS and CWM (Table 12) may systematically favor classification. Did you consider that generation tasks may require different training dynamics?\n\n3. **Input context parity.** Have you tested CWM with longer input context (e.g., training with max_seq_length=4096 or 8192, reserving 1024 for generation)? The current setup gives CLS 4-8x more input context than CWM at repo level. The ablation only shows CLS is robust to truncation, not that CWM would not benefit from more context.\n\n4. **Re-repair experiment.** The experiment plan references a diagnostic re-repair experiment. Was it conducted? If so, why are the results not reported? If the result was negative, that is informative and should be disclosed.\n\n5. **DeepSeek results.** The experiment plan and report reference DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B experiments. Why are these excluded from the main paper? Do they confirm or contradict the Qwen3 findings? The non-monotonic gap trend across architectures mentioned in the plan is concerning.\n\n6. **CWM at scale.** Your CWM is a LoRA-tuned 4B/8B model. The original CWM is a 32B mid-trained model. Do you have any evidence that your findings would hold for a properly-resourced generation model? Without this, the claim that \"generation stalls\" (per the title) may be specific to low-resource LoRA fine-tuning.\n\n7. **Unparseable default assignment.** You default unparseable CWM outputs to \"pass.\" Have you tested the sensitivity of your results to this choice (e.g., defaulting to \"fail,\" or to a random prediction)? The choice of default materially affects the reported accuracy numbers.\n\n### 5. Score and Confidence\n\n**Score: 3 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Confidence: 4 (High)**\n\n**Justification.** The paper addresses a useful research question and the function-level experiments are adequately controlled. However, the repository-level results---which carry the paper's most dramatic claims (19-26pp gaps, \"generation stalls\")---rest on single-seed comparisons with documented extreme seed variance, structurally unfair resource allocation between CLS and CWM, and a CWM training regime that shows clear signs of being poorly optimized. The omission of the negative re-repair experiment, the selective reporting of seed-42-best results, and the generalization from a single model family to broad formulation claims are significant methodological concerns. The paper would benefit from: (a) multi-seed repo-level results with proper variance reporting, (b) CWM training with input context parity, (c) inclusion of DeepSeek results and the re-repair experiment, and (d) more cautious framing that distinguishes \"LoRA-tuned CWM at 4-8B underperforms\" from \"generation as a formulation is fundamentally flawed.\""}],"totalDurationMs":113131,"totalTokens":48671,"totalToolUseCount":12,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8390,"cache_read_input_tokens":37144,"output_tokens":3136,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8390},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3136,"cache_read_input_tokens":37144,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8390,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8390,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":12,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
